| `09_Archive/` | Superseded instructions retained for context |

## B. Whole-project purpose

Develop a lightweight CFRP panel that manages internally generated heat while reducing solar heat gain. In the Word guide, the outside-to-inside stack is **BN/CNF/MTMS surface → upper CFRP → CNT/CNF/MTMS interlayer → lower CFRP**, with a test heater at the back. BN supports solar reflection; the CNT-containing region is investigated for heat transport. Porosity and alignment control directional thermal behaviour. Integration, resin infiltration and interfaces can change the dry aerogel's properties.

## C. Experiment requirements

Read `02_Experiment/Experiment_Guide_original.docx` for materials, placement, fabrication, sample matrix and thermal/optical tests. Its text extraction and issue register sit beside it. The guide remains unchanged. Conflicting or unclear dimensions, layer counts and formulation bases are listed in the extracted requirements; do not silently choose between them.

## D. Work already done for the build agent

I have translated and context-corrected the full supplied transcript and extracted its requirements into `04_Simulation/Requirements_Extracted_From_Transcript.md`. **The receiving agent's job is to build the simulator from these requirements.** Begin with aerogel alone, a simple aligned pore geometry, fixed composition and a pore-size sweep. Then investigate composition and directional conductivity. CFRP integration follows later. Read `04_Simulation/Build_Brief.md` for implementation details.

## E. Reference basis

Use **Lou et al. (2023), “Thermal Insulation Performance of Aerogel Nano-Porous Materials: Characterization and Test Methods,” DOI 10.3390/gels9030220**, as the main reference, **including but not limited to it**. See `05_References/START_HERE.md` for supporting mathematical models, directional BN/CNF studies and Kit's papers. Added reference notes are included offline. Full-PDF availability is explicit: the retained Chan paper is included; other papers currently have verified links and notes because downloads were blocked.
